// OS thread creation: the primitive a scheduler starts its worker pool with,
// and the bounded join that reclaims one.
//
// Stage-2 port of `runtime/sched.zig`'s `Scheduler.start` — the two lines that
// call `std.Thread.spawn` per worker and `join()` on the way out. Both exist
// during the port; the scheduler port (#1364) is what consumes this.
//
// This module is the OTHER half of #1403. Its first half (`runtime/park`)
// shipped the primitive an idle worker WAITS on; this one ships the primitive
// that creates the worker in the first place. That half stopped at a real
// boundary — both platforms need a function's code entry address to start a
// thread and Bit had no expression yielding one — which #1404 has since closed
// with `entryOf(f): *byte` (SPEC §11.10).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE SEAM AGAINST #1364
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This module owns "make an OS thread run this function, and wait for it to
// finish". It does NOT own run queues, work stealing, the context switch, the
// idle-worker state machine, or any decision about WHEN to start or park a
// worker. Those are #1364's, and deciding them here would be the same mistake
// the parking half declined to make.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT A CHILD THREAD MAY DO — READ THIS BEFORE WRITING ONE
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A thread created here is NOT a registered runtime worker. It has no green
// task, no scheduler TLS, and the collector does not know it exists. Three
// consequences, each load-bearing:
//
//  1. IT MUST NOT ALLOCATE. The collector is stop-the-world with respect to
//     "the one thread that runs Bit code" (ABI.md §5); a second thread
//     allocating concurrently races the mutator the collector believes is
//     quiescent. Under `BIT_GC=stress` — which collects at every safepoint —
//     that race is not theoretical.
//
//  2. IT MUST REACH NO SAFEPOINT POLL. Codegen emits `bl bit_rt_safepoint`
//     at LOOP BACK-EDGES only (seed/codegen/arm64.zig's module doc), and
//     `@nosplit` suppresses exactly that. So the rule with teeth is: the entry
//     function and everything it calls must either be `@nosplit`, or be
//     LOOP-FREE by inspection. The parking providers' leaves qualify on the
//     second clause — `parkWake`/`parkSleepNs` contain no loop — which is what
//     lets a child signal its parent at all. `parkCpuCount` does contain a
//     loop and is therefore NOT callable from a child.
//
//  3. ON DARWIN IT MUST NOT USE `!`/`fail`/`?`. Those lower to a thread-local
//     error slot, so they need thread-local storage the child owns.
//
//     ON LINUX IT MAY (#1417): `./linux` sets `CLONE_SETTLS` and hands the
//     kernel a per-thread block it builds from this image's own `PT_TLS`
//     geometry, so a child gets its own thread pointer and its own error slot.
//     `@threadlocal` (§11.11) is equally usable there, which is what #1364 needs
//     — `currentTask()`, `currentScheduler()` and the collector all start from
//     per-worker thread-local state. See ./linux/tls.bit for how the geometry is
//     derived rather than assumed.
//
//     ON DARWIN the restriction stands: `@threadlocal` is refused for Mach-O
//     altogether (§11.11 — a TLV access CALLS a resolver thunk rather than doing
//     arithmetic off a thread pointer), and the provider's `pthread_create`
//     already gives its child libSystem-managed TLS that this module does not
//     control. Nothing here has to build a block, and nothing here may assume
//     one has the ELF layout.
//
// These are restrictions on the CHILD, not on the parent. The parent is an
// ordinary registered thread and may do anything.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE PLATFORM SEAM
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Same forced split as `runtime/alloc` (#1362), `runtime/rand` (#1368) and
// `runtime/park` (#1403's first half): `syscall` (§11.8) is Linux-only and
// `extern function` (§11.7) is Darwin-only, and BOTH reject even an uncalled
// declaration, so no `hostTarget()` branch can carry the two paths in one file.
// A module is a directory (§17.1), so:
//
//   runtime/thread/          this file: the join loop and the shared contract
//   runtime/thread/linux/    clone(2) onto a guarded mmap'd stack
//   runtime/thread/darwin/   pthread_create via libSystem
//
// This file imports NEITHER provider — it holds no platform code at all — and
// the consumer binds the pair it wants.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE ONE CONTRACT BOTH PROVIDERS IMPLEMENT
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   threadStart(entry, arg, done) -> handle    handle > 0, or 0 on failure
//   threadRelease(handle)         -> bool      after `threadJoin` reports done
//
// `entry` is `entryOf(f)` for a named `function f(arg: *byte): *byte` — the C
// entry shape, because that is what `pthread_create` requires and what the
// Linux child's register-indirect branch hands its argument in. `done` is a
// caller-owned word (see `threadRunning`).
//
// `handle` is DELIBERATELY OPAQUE, and it is not the same thing on the two
// platforms — the Linux stack mapping's base address, Darwin's `pthread_t`.
// Making it opaque is what lets one contract cover both: the caller never needs
// the tid, and the thing that actually has to be released differs. A caller
// that reads meaning into the number is wrong on one platform or the other.

import { parkMinBackoffNs, parkNextBackoff } from "../park"

// The value a caller stores into its `done` word BEFORE calling `threadStart`,
// and which becomes 0 when the thread has finished.
//
// WHO CLEARS IT DIFFERS BY PLATFORM, and the difference is a real one worth
// knowing rather than papering over:
//
//   - Linux: THE KERNEL clears it, via `CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID`. That is the
//     strong form — it happens during thread teardown, after the child has
//     left user space for good, so seeing 0 proves the stack is no longer in
//     use and may be unmapped.
//   - Darwin: THE CHILD clears it, because pthreads offers no equivalent. That
//     is the weaker form: the thread may still be executing its last few
//     instructions of libSystem teardown. It does not matter, because on Darwin
//     the stack belongs to pthreads and `threadRelease` reaps through
//     `pthread_join`, which has its own guarantee.
//
// Any nonzero sentinel works; 1 is Zig's (`child_tid: .init(1)` in
// std/Thread.zig's `LinuxThreadImpl`).
export const threadRunning: int = 1

// `threadJoin` saw the thread finish.
export const threadJoinDone: int = 0

// `threadJoin` gave up: the deadline passed with the thread still running. This
// is a REPORTED OUTCOME, not an error to be swallowed — it is what makes every
// join finite by construction. A join that could block forever would turn a
// stuck worker into a hung test suite instead of a failing one, which is both a
// Power-of-10 violation (a loop with no provable bound) and strictly worse to
// debug. This project has already paid for that lesson once, when a closing
// QUIC peer stranded its waiters and the suite hung rather than failed.
export const threadJoinTimedOut: int = 1

// The default stack size for a worker thread, in bytes.
//
// 16MiB is `std.Thread.SpawnConfig.default_stack_size` (verified in the pinned
// stdlib's Thread.zig, not recalled), which is what `sched.zig`'s
// `std.Thread.spawn(.{}, Worker.run, .{w})` gets by taking the default config.
// So this is behavioural equivalence, not a fresh choice.
//
// It costs almost nothing: the mapping is anonymous and lazily faulted, so a
// worker occupies address space rather than memory. A worker's own stack stays
// shallow in any case — a green thread runs on the TASK's stack, not this one.
export const threadStackBytes: int = 16777216

// The upper bound on workers, mirrored from `Scheduler.init`'s own assertion
// (`n > 0 and n <= 1024`). Named here because it belongs with the thread
// primitive rather than being re-derived by each consumer.
export const threadMaxWorkers: int = 1024

// The static trip bound on `threadJoin`'s poll loop.
//
// The DEADLINE is the operative bound in every real use — this exists so that
// the loop's termination is provable by READING it, without reasoning about the
// clock at all (Power of 10: every loop has a statically provable limit). A
// deadline alone is not a bound, because it is only as trustworthy as the clock
// enforcing it: a stopped clock would make a deadline-only loop run forever,
// turning a stuck worker into a hung suite instead of a failing one.
// tests/stress/thread's T2 drives exactly that case.
//
// The backoff saturates at `parkMaxBackoffNs` = 1ms, so this many polls
// corresponds to roughly 100 seconds of waiting. That is far beyond any join
// deadline a scheduler would set — worker shutdown is milliseconds — so in
// practice the deadline always wins. A caller that genuinely wants to wait
// longer than that calls again; whichever bound is reached first, the answer
// returned is the same `threadJoinTimedOut`.
const JOIN_MAX_POLLS: int = 100000

// Wait for a thread to finish, for at most `timeoutNs`. Returns
// `threadJoinDone` or `threadJoinTimedOut`.
//
// This is `std.Thread.join()`'s role in `Scheduler.start`'s error path and in
// shutdown, with one deviation and one deliberate simplification.
//
// THE DEVIATION — WHY THIS POLLS RATHER THAN FUTEX-WAITING. Zig's Linux join
// blocks in `futex(FUTEX_WAIT)` on the child-tid word, woken by the kernel's
// `CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID` teardown. It cannot be reused here, and the reason is
// specific rather than stylistic: Zig issues that wait with `.private = false`,
// because the kernel performs the clear-child-tid wake as a SHARED futex
// operation. `runtime/park`'s `parkWait` is `FUTEX_PRIVATE` by construction —
// private and shared waits key their queues differently, so a private waiter
// would not be woken by the kernel's shared wake and the join would hang until
// its deadline on every single call. Polling sidesteps the mismatch entirely,
// and it is also what `sched.zig` already does everywhere else it waits: its
// idle path is bounded exponential backoff, explicitly NOT a futex (see
// ../park/park.bit). So polling here is the consistent choice, not a lesser one.
//
// THE SIMPLIFICATION — no join-once bookkeeping. Zig's `Thread.join` tracks a
// completion state so a double join is a detectable programming error. Reading
// a word that is already 0 simply returns `threadJoinDone` again, which is the
// idempotent answer and needs no state to produce.
//
// The clock and sleep are taken as function VALUES rather than imported,
// because this file must import neither provider (see the header) and both are
// platform code. It is the same shape `tests/stress/park/props` uses to run one
// copy of a check against either platform, and it keeps ONE join loop in the
// tree instead of a copy per provider that could drift apart.
export function threadJoin(done: *i32, timeoutNs: int,
                           sleepFn: (int) => (),
                           monoFn: () => int): int {
  // A non-positive timeout still gets one look at the word, so a join on an
  // already-finished thread succeeds rather than reporting a spurious timeout.
  if (atomicLoad(done) == 0) {
    return threadJoinDone
  }
  let deadline = monoFn() + timeoutNs
  let backoff = parkMinBackoffNs
  let i = 0
  while (i < JOIN_MAX_POLLS) {
    if (atomicLoad(done) == 0) {
      return threadJoinDone
    }
    if (monoFn() >= deadline) {
      return threadJoinTimedOut
    }
    sleepFn(backoff)
    backoff = parkNextBackoff(backoff)
    i = i + 1
  }
  return threadJoinTimedOut
}
